                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="725-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_wc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Wide Cardioid" title="Wide Cardioid" ></span>Kel Audio HM1</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Wide Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Kel HM-1 is an inexpensive small-diaphragm FET condenser with a reputation for producing a neutral-to-dark sound. The mic is intended to provide a contrast to the hyped HF commonly found in imported SDCs.</p><p>Despite the large body style and vertical capsule mounting, the capsule measures only 1/2&#39;&#39; in diameter. The capsule (diameter 20mm, with ~12.5mm diaphragm) is shockmounted within the mic body.</p><p>Each mic includes a frequency graph showing the individual response of the microphone. </p><p>No pads or switches are provided.</p><p>We believe the HM-1, like all the Kel Audio mics, employs a low-noise, transformerless output circuit.</p><blockquote><p><cite>ProRec.com</cite></p><p>a dark sounding microphone, much like a high-frequency-damped version of the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/AT4050">Audio Technica 4050</a>, and with a similar output level&#8230;</p><p>The HM-1 does tom duty very well &#8212; plenty of low-end &#8220;doooom,&#8221; and enough stick attack to give good definition to the drum.</p></blockquote><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/kel/HM-1_internal.jpg" width="99" alt="Kel Audio HM-1" class="thumb shaft" /></p><blockquote><p><cite>Recording Magazine</cite></p><p>[T]he smaller capsule size and sonic characteristics will not make the HM-1 your &#8220;go to&#8221; mic, especially with quiet sources. The smaller capsule in conjunction with the compact casing is better suited for warming up harsh or bright instruments, or as an alternative to loading up a mix with multiple tracks that all exhibit the same crisp and potentially brittle top end&#8230;</p><p>[T]he HM-1 doesn&#8217;t muddy things up. It captures the source in a very flat and natural way.</p></blockquote><p>The mic ships with a shockmount and a plastic carrying case.</p><p>As of 2011, the HM1 has been replaced by the HM-1x, an upgraded version of the same basic microphone.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Kel-Audio/HM1">Kel Audio HM1</a> is also known as: HM-1.</p><p>The mic was released in 2004.</p>
